---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="A Practical Guide to REST API Design"
  description="Core REST API design decisions that are hard to change later — idempotency, versioning, error formats, and rate limiting — with checklists and generators for each."
>
  <div class="max-w-2xl mx-auto px-4 py-16 prose prose-slate dark:prose-invert">
    <p class="text-sm text-emerald-600 dark:text-emerald-400 font-medium mb-2">Guide</p>
    <h1>A Practical Guide to REST API Design</h1>
    <p>
      Most REST API design mistakes aren't discovered during development — they surface months
      later, once external consumers depend on the API and a fix would break existing integrations.
      This guide covers the decisions worth getting right early, before anyone else is relying on
      your endpoints.
    </p>

    <h2>Use HTTP methods and status codes as intended</h2>
    <p>
      REST's biggest advantage is predictability — a consumer who understands HTTP can guess how
      your API behaves without reading documentation for every endpoint, but only if you actually
      follow the conventions. GET should never have side effects; POST creates; PUT replaces; DELETE
      removes. The <a href="/tools/http-methods-reference">HTTP Methods Reference</a> covers
      idempotency and safety for each method, which directly affects whether a client can safely
      retry a failed request.
    </p>

    <h2>Decide your idempotency story before you need it</h2>
    <p>
      Retrying a timed-out request is safe for idempotent operations (GET, PUT, DELETE) but
      dangerous for non-idempotent ones like POST, where a naive retry can create a duplicate order
      or double-charge a payment. The
      <a href="/tools/idempotency-reference">Idempotent vs Non-Idempotent Operations Reference</a>
      breaks down which methods are safe by default, and for the ones that aren't, an
      <a href="/tools/idempotency-key-generator">idempotency key</a> lets clients retry safely by
      having the server recognize and deduplicate repeated requests.
    </p>

    <h2>Pick a versioning strategy before your first breaking change</h2>
    <p>
      Once external consumers depend on v1, changing how you version — URL path versus header,
      deprecation timeline — becomes a breaking change in itself. Decide this upfront using the
      <a href="/tools/versioning-checklist">API Versioning Strategy Checklist</a>, and when you do
      need to deprecate an old version, signal it properly with the
      <a href="/tools/deprecation-header-generator">API Deprecation Header Generator</a> rather than
      relying on a changelog post alone.
    </p>

    <h2>Return errors in a consistent, predictable shape</h2>
    <p>
      An API where every endpoint's error response looks slightly different forces client code to
      special-case each one. Establish one error format — code, message, and field-level details
      where relevant — using the
      <a href="/tools/error-format-generator">API Error Response Format Generator</a>, then apply it
      consistently across every endpoint, not just the ones you happen to write last.
    </p>

    <h2>Communicate rate limits, don't just enforce them silently</h2>
    <p>
      A client that hits a rate limit with no signal beyond a bare 429 has no way to behave
      intelligently — it can only guess how long to wait. Returning proper
      <a href="/tools/rate-limit-headers-reference">rate limit headers</a> (remaining requests, reset
      time) lets well-behaved clients pace themselves proactively instead of hitting the limit
      repeatedly through trial and error.
    </p>

    <h2>Review against a full checklist before shipping v1</h2>
    <p>
      Individual decisions are easier to get right in isolation than to remember all at once under
      deadline pressure. Run through the
      <a href="/tools/api-design-checklist">REST API Design Checklist</a> before your first public
      release — it's much cheaper to fix a naming inconsistency or missing status code before anyone
      is depending on the current behavior.
    </p>

